# Memory findings that are nginx's own, not this module's.  The valgrind job
# drives the module through ci/upload.sh, which runs one foreground nginx
# (master_process off) end to end and shuts it down with QUIT, so the pools
# are freed and whatever is left here belongs to nginx core, not to upload.
#
# Every entry is measured, not assumed: with an empty file the driver reports
# exactly these three and nothing else, and in none of them does a frame from
# ngx_http_upload_module.c appear -- the leaks are nginx start-up arrays it
# keeps until the process exits, and the conditional jump reads bytes that
# --track-origins traces to nginx's own request pool (ngx_http_process_
# request_line -> ngx_list_init), not to anything the module allocated.
#
# Each entry is pinned to the nginx core functions it occurs in, so it cannot
# mask the same class of finding if it ever came from the module instead.  If
# a later nginx brings a new finding, valgrind prints a full trace; add it
# then, with a name and a reason, rather than keeping one here on spec.

{
   nginx keeps the environment array until the process ends
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:malloc
   fun:ngx_alloc
   fun:ngx_set_environment
   ...
}

{
   nginx keeps the connection and event arrays until the process ends
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   fun:malloc
   fun:ngx_alloc
   fun:ngx_event_process_init
   ...
}

{
   nginx core reads its own request-pool bytes walking the busy output chain
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:ngx_chain_update_chains
   fun:ngx_output_chain
   fun:ngx_http_upstream_send_request_body
   ...
}
